  "body": "### Summary\n\nAs a user of an iPad or iPhone with Touch ID (or with a passcode only), I want the app-lock switch to name the method my device has, so that the setting does not promise Face ID where there is none.\n\n### Rule\n\n\"Refer only to authentication methods that are available in the current context. For example, don't reference Face ID on a device that doesn't offer it. Check the device's capabilities and use the appropriate terminology. For developer guidance, see LABiometryType.\" — [HIG › Managing accounts](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/managing-accounts) (Best practices).\n\n### What the app does (dev-swift @ `7a61018f`)\n\n`App/SwiftHFR/Settings/SecuritySettingsSections.swift:35`: `Toggle(\"Verrouiller l'app (Face ID / code)\", isOn: …)`: a fixed string. `grep -rn \"biometryType\" App/SwiftHFR` finds nothing.\n\n### Legacy at `87fe0d1c`\n\nNo counterpart: the app lock is new in SwiftHFR (LOT-173, GAP-168/169).\n\n### Class\n\n**(a) defect** (SwiftHFR-only; also an App Review risk on Touch ID iPads).\n\n### Proposed fix and check\n\n- Read `LAContext().biometryType` after `canEvaluatePolicy` and build the label from it (« Face ID », « Touch ID », « Optic ID », or « code » alone for `.none`), behind the app-lock model's existing seam so it is testable.\n- Check: a unit test of the label for each `LABiometryType`. Mac check: a Touch ID simulator (iPad) shows « Touch ID ».\n\nFound by #866 (cloud/r866-fable, Fable 5.2)",
